    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d125">

                <pb facs="0264" n="170" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg170"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.348 Z 4/45: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Whitehouse</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-09-28T19:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 28,
                                1923—7 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:10
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>393. Reference Department’s unnumbered despatch of September 18, flight of
                    Zeppelin to New Jersey.</p>
                <p>Before action is taken on instruction referred to, may I suggest that from here
                    the safer course and one less likely to raise difficulties <pb facs="0265"
                        n="171" xml:id="pg171"/> would be to wait until the route is definitely
                    determined and then to approach the Government or Governments concerned, taking
                    the attitude of assuming as a matter of course that as necessary consequence of
                    Allied Powers having consented to construction of our Zeppelin, authorization
                    for its flight will be given.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitehouse</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
